ribute a product that links to or bundles `somelib`, the combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0, source included. That conflicts with shipping closed source.\n- **How you ship matters.** Distributing binaries, a desktop or mobile app, or an on-prem install triggers the obligations. A hosted SaaS usually doesn't count as distribution under GPL-3.0, but that's a judgment call. If `somelib` were AGPL, SaaS would trigger it too.\n- **GPL-3.0 also adds anti-tivoization and patent-license terms.** These make it harder to live with than GPL-2.0 in embedded or locked-down devices.\n\n**Options, roughly in order of preference:**\n1. Replace `somelib` with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, Apache-2.0 or BSD).\n2. Ask the author for a commercial or dual license.\n3. Isolate it as a separate process that talks over a CLI or network boundary. Have counsel review this first, because it's legally gray.\n4. Open-source your product under GPL-3.0.\n\n**The other two are fine:**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0) and `left-pad` (MIT) are permissive. You need to include their license text and copyright notices in your attribution or NOTICE file. Apache-2.0 also requires you to state any changes you made to the library.\n\n**Other things to check:**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies can carry GPL or AGPL licenses too, so scan the full resolved tree.\n- The license strings in the file are self-reported. Confirm them against each package's actual metadata and LICENSE file.\n- `requests==2.32` looks like a truncated version. Pin the exact release, such as 2.32.x, so the license and security posture are reproducible.\n- `left-pad` is an npm package, while `requests` is Python. If the product mixes ecosystems, check that both trees are covered.\n\nPlease have legal counsel confirm before release, since I'm not a lawyer. I can also run the license-scan skill against the full resolved tree if you'd like.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", 